WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:45.520
NO REGRETS:
“It was well worth doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:51.120
prison time for The Pirate Bay.” This was the&nbsp;
final battle cry of Pirate Bay cofounder Fredrick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.960
Neij despite getting raided by the police multiple&nbsp;
times, being fined 46 million kroner, and being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.400
thrown in prison alongside some of the most vile&nbsp;
people on Earth. In fact, Fredrick was the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.560
person in his entire prison that was there for a&nbsp;
virtual crime. Everyone else there was convicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:14.640
of robbery, assault, manslaughter, or worse. But,&nbsp;
things were not too bad in prison says Fredrick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.360
He immersed himself with reading letters from&nbsp;
fans about how much the Pirate Bay meant to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.400
This isn’t to say that Fredrick willing turned&nbsp;
himself in though. Like any normal human being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.320
he very much dreaded the idea of going to&nbsp;
jail and he was on the run for several years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:35.560
as one of the most hated individuals by media&nbsp;
organizations around the world. But, eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.680
the law did catch up with him putting him behind&nbsp;
bars. But, this only strengthened Fredrick’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.200
personal conviction as the letters reminded him&nbsp;
of why did everything in the first place. Unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.800
other piracy website creators, Fredrick wasn’t&nbsp;
trying to make money or get rich or screw over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.400
big companies. He had founded The Pirate Bay out&nbsp;
of principle. The idea that information, culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.560
and knowledge should be shared freely around the&nbsp;
world without the interference of corporate greed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.160
bureaucracy, or political influence. In fact,&nbsp;
Fredrick had been fighting for this cause long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.520
before he co founded the Pirate Bay or gained any&nbsp;
sort of notoriety. Before his Pirate Bay days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.160
Fredrick was part of a Swedish anti copyright&nbsp;
group that went around the world engaging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.560
piracy debates and giving lectures about&nbsp;
the right to information. This is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.920
Fredrick met the other founders Gottfrid&nbsp;
Svartholm and Peter Sunde. And together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:36.600
they would go on to create one of the most&nbsp;
controversial yet legendary sites of all time. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:42.080
here’s how the Pirate Bay went from being an&nbsp;
ideological effort to becoming an international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:55.920
icon despite police raids, jail time, and&nbsp;
the efforts of the media industry at large.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.840
THE SCALLYWAGS:
The story of the Pirate Bay dates back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.480
to the late 1970s and early 1980s to the country&nbsp;
of Sweden where all three of our protagonists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.280
were born. Not much is known about any of the&nbsp;
founders’ childhoods, but from what we do know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:16.320
it wasn’t anything to brag about. Fredrick was the&nbsp;
oldest of the bunch having been born on April 27,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:22.560
1978 in the city of Jönköping Sweden. There’s&nbsp;
rumors that he dropped out of high school and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.760
it appears that he just stumbled though his life&nbsp;
one day at at time. A similar story could be said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.720
about Gottfrid as well who was the youngest&nbsp;
of the bunch having been born on October 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:39.240
1984. Gottfrid was your classic computer&nbsp;
nerd/hacker of the 1990s and 2000s. Think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.240
Dennis Nedry from Jurassic Park. And then&nbsp;
there was Peter Sunde who was the only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.120
that would be considered somewhat successful&nbsp;
by modern society, but this wasn’t to say the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:56.360
his childhood was any better. Peter was born on&nbsp;
September 13, 1978 in Uddevalla Sweden. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:01.640
he life wasn’t all that bad. His mother was&nbsp;
a staff consultant at a large company and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:07.120
his father was a traveling mechanic. But, all of&nbsp;
this fell apart when Peter’s parents got a divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.360
when he was just 8 years old. His mother would&nbsp;
become clinically delusional as she developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:18.400
psychosis which resulted in the family constantly&nbsp;
moving all across Scandanavia. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.120
Peter would end up dropping out of school in&nbsp;
his teens, but there was one saving grace:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.600
computers. Computers were Peter’s one and only&nbsp;
escape from his harsh realty and this led to him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.000
becoming quite a skilled programmer. Ironically,&nbsp;
if he had this same childhood in modern times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.160
it’s likely that he would’ve become a lifeless&nbsp;
social media addict like myse..Anyways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.640
things were different back then. The people&nbsp;
spending all of their time on computers weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:50.720
endlessly scrolling through TikTok. They were&nbsp;
enthusiasts learning how to code and hack systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.320
and this would catch the attention of German&nbsp;
technology company Siemens. Siemens is the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:02.040
behind many of the IT systems used at hospitals,&nbsp;
and they would hire Peter to upgrade their patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.160
registration system from being fingerprint based&nbsp;
to being retinal based. It seemed like a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:13.160
good gig at first glance, but Peter could not&nbsp;
stand it at Siemens after he joined. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.800
discover that his development efforts were being&nbsp;
used to identify and deport illegal immigrants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:22.880
leading them to do unthinkable things. You see,&nbsp;
many of these immigrants would burn their fingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.760
on kitchen stoves before visiting the hospital to&nbsp;
avoid being identified. This was actually one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:34.480
the main reasons that Siemens wanted to switch&nbsp;
to a retinal system. But this simply gave Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.280
nightmares about immigrants poking out their&nbsp;
eyes like Victor Creel. He tried to take it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.520
up with his managers, but this simply gave him&nbsp;
the title of being negative and difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.160
work with. Being drained from work everyday,&nbsp;
the only solace Peter found was during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:59.480
evenings when he scrolled through online forums&nbsp;
leading him to discover the world of piracy.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.960
FOUNDING THE PIRATE BAY:
Peter’s piracy journey started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.720
with him swapping music and movies on invite only&nbsp;
servers which led him to meeting fellow scallywag:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.560
Fredrick Neij. Fredrick introduced Peter to the&nbsp;
file-sharing movement and to the newly founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.680
anti copyright organization: Piratbyrån or The&nbsp;
Pirate Bureau. Piratbyrån was founded on August 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:27.080
2003 by a bunch of societal outcasts just like&nbsp;
Peter, Fredrick, and Gottfrid. Their goal was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:32.320
to make information available to people of all&nbsp;
backgrounds and stature, and promote free speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.320
above everythign else. Not saying I agree with&nbsp;
how they went about doing this, but honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.880
it does seem like their intentions were way more&nbsp;
pure than most of the people who actually control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.440
the world today. With this idea in mind, the&nbsp;
group got to work. Some members tried opposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:54.800
pro copyright lobbyist groups. Others traveled the&nbsp;
world and gave lectures on radio and TV. Our trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:07:01.240
decided to create a website. This was specifically&nbsp;
Fredrick’s idea. He wanted to create a Bittorrent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:06.600
tracker and he would recruit the help of Peter&nbsp;
and Gottfrid for the IT and coding side of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.000
Given the relative simplicity of the website,&nbsp;
Peter and Gottfrid had this up and running in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:18.440
no time and Piratbyrån would launch the website on&nbsp;
September 15, 2003. This was actually just one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:24.693
many websites that this trio launched. Moving into&nbsp;
2004, Fredrick and Gottfrid would create a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.693 --> 00:07:30.480
on internet service provider and web hosting&nbsp;
company called PRQ. Don’t be fooled though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:37.280
this was no AT&amp;T. With PRQ, Fredrick and Gottfrid&nbsp;
looked to give an online voice to anyone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:44.280
everyone regardless of what they stood for or did.&nbsp;
This included some unheard and repressed voices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:50.720
but it also included a bunch of creeps literally.&nbsp;
There were a bunch of forums hosted on PRQ that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.280
advocated for how should I say it: unorthodox&nbsp;
relationships. Though Fredrick and Gottfrid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:01.040
definitely did not agree with everything that&nbsp;
was hosted on their platform, they defended all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.120
of their customers equally citing freedom speech.&nbsp;
Something else that sparked a bunch of controversy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:12.600
was yet another one this duo’s ventures: Americas&nbsp;
Dumbest Soldiers. Honestly, this one was quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:18.360
distasteful. The website consisted of a list of&nbsp;
deceased American soldiers and it prompted people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:24.280
to vote on how dumb their deaths were. Little&nbsp;
to say, this site didn’t stay up for very long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:28.880
While these other ventures definitely caused&nbsp;
a scene by themselves, it turned out that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:33.920
trio’s original venture would have the greatest&nbsp;
sticking power. And luckily for them, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.600
pretty cheap to run. Afterall, the Pirate Bay&nbsp;
didn’t actually have any file hosting or server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:45.000
to client downloading abilites built in. The site&nbsp;
simply connected people with a library of links&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.960
and all of the downloading took place using peer&nbsp;
to peer protocols. So, the trio just had to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:54.520
that they had enough server capacity to handle&nbsp;
all of the search volume, indexing, sorting, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.520 --> 00:09:07.301
filtering itself. But, to their surprise it didn’t&nbsp;
take long for this itself to become unmanageable.

00:09:07.301 --> 00:09:10.120
THE LEGENDARY RISE:
As the Pirate Bay exploded it popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:15.640
it became abundantly clear that this could&nbsp;
no longer be treated as a side project. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:22.160
in October of 2004, the trio would transition the&nbsp;
site into being a standalone non profit based in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:27.200
the Seychelles, though this is disputed.&nbsp;
If you also have no idea what this place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:32.440
you’re not alone. The Seychelles is an East&nbsp;
African country that’s composed of a 115&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:38.240
islands and it just so happened to be the perfect&nbsp;
place to setup a shell company. Now, they had an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:44.800
entity. All they needed was infrastructure. Enter&nbsp;
Carl Lundström. This guy was one of five heirs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.840
the Wasabrod fortune. I’ve actually never heard&nbsp;
of this company, but they’re super big. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:55.960
extremely popular across Scandinavia and they’re&nbsp;
actually the largest crisp bread producer in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:01.560
world. By the time Carl turned 22, this company&nbsp;
would be sold to a Swiss pharmaceutical company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.960
called Sandoz which made Carl a mega millionaire&nbsp;
by the time most of us would even graduate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:13.080
college. Carl would turnaround and use this wealth&nbsp;
to fund a variety of extreme political movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.960
and it wasn’t long till he crossed paths with&nbsp;
our protagonists. He would agree to provide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:23.160
Pirate Bay with all of the required servers and&nbsp;
infrastructure and before you knew it, the Pirate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:28.640
Bay would become a fully funded venture making&nbsp;
copyright holders around the world cry. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:34.440
they weren’t crying for long. They were hurt&nbsp;
and they were hurt bad. Hollywood claims that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:42.840
in 2006 alone, piracy cost them $6.1 billion.&nbsp;
And the biggest culprit enabling this asinine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.640
behavior was the Swedish site that was just one&nbsp;
Google search away: the Pirate Bay. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:54.400
Hollywood wanted revenge, they wanted justice.&nbsp;
So, they marched over to the Swedish authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.400 --> 00:11:00.680
and demanded that the Pirate Bay be taken down as&nbsp;
soon as possible. This was met with a chuckle from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:07.360
Sweden. I’m not even kidding. You see, Sweden is&nbsp;
surprisingly chill about copyright and patents. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.760
fact, they have a full on political party called&nbsp;
the Pirate Party who’s entire focus is to reform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:18.320
copyright law. Hollywood was gobsmacked and they&nbsp;
would take their grievances to the good old USA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.640
Surely, their hundreds of millions of lobbying&nbsp;
dollars would actually earn them some respect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:29.120
the American authorities and they were right.&nbsp;
The US would turn around and go “Yo Sweden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:33.160
you better shutdown those mischievous rascals&nbsp;
you got there. You don’t want us to bring out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:38.200
the stick and impose trade sanctions through the&nbsp;
World Trade Organizations right?” And to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:43.480
Sweden would respond “Your wish is our command”&nbsp;
and they would go ahead and raid the Pirate Bay’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:49.640
servers in Stockholm on May 31, 2006. There was&nbsp;
so much equipment that the Swedish police would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:54.680
end up filling three trucks worth of computer&nbsp;
equipment and servers. Surely, this would put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.680 --> 00:12:10.097
an end to the nonsense they thought. Ummm, the&nbsp;
Pirate Bay would be back up in just 3 days.

00:12:10.097 --> 00:12:12.560
HOLLYWOOD STRIKES BACK:
You’re probably wondering, how was the trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:19.400
able to restart the Pirate Bay so quickly? Well,&nbsp;
the answer is that the Pirate Bay is nothing. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:24.560
really just a massive excel sheet with a bunch of&nbsp;
links. In fact, the entirety of the Pirate Bay can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:30.360
be compressed into a 90 megabyte zip file. So,&nbsp;
they didn’t have to maintain massive convoluted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:35.320
code bases and repositories or enterprise&nbsp;
resource planning software. All of the servers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:40.760
and equipment were simply there to support the&nbsp;
extraordinary traffic. So, as soon as they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:45.320
able to find new infrastructure, they were able&nbsp;
to upload their tiny little zip file and go live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:51.400
in no time. This time, they chose to put their&nbsp;
servers in a more discrete location somewhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:56.960
the Netherlands so that the police couldn’t just&nbsp;
turn around and raid them again. Hollywood was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:02.840
triggered. Not only was the Pirate Bay back up and&nbsp;
running, but the police raid had simply drawn even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:08.720
more attention to the website. Clearly, Hollywood&nbsp;
didn’t have much power in Sweden, so they turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:14.360
their focus to American companies. They might&nbsp;
not be able to shutdown the Pirate Bay, but maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:19.480
they could prevent you from accessing it. They&nbsp;
started working with Google, Facebook, Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:25.160
and ISPs to suppress the sharing and searching&nbsp;
of Pirate Bay links, but this only made things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:30.560
worse. Here’s the thing, when you tell people&nbsp;
not to do something, that’s when they want to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:35.120
it even more and this couldn’t be any truer with&nbsp;
the Pirate Bay. Just because Facebook or Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:40.360
suppressed Pirate Bay links doesn’t mean jack. You&nbsp;
don’t have to be a rocket scientist to go directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:46.880
to the Pirate Bay and search up whatever you want,&nbsp;
and that was exactly what was happening. By 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:52.800
the Pirate Bay became the 97th most visited&nbsp;
website on the entire internet, and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:59.600
even having talks about selling the website to&nbsp;
Global Gaming Factory X AB for $8.5 million. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:05.720
was becoming abundantly clear that there was only&nbsp;
one way to stop this madness: strike the tent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:12.360
On January 31, 2008, Swedish prosecutors would&nbsp;
file charges against Gottfrid, Fredrick, Peter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:17.400
and Carl. From the very beginning, this was an&nbsp;
open and shut case. The Pirate Bay’s lawyers tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:23.320
to defend that the Pirate Bay was simply hosting&nbsp;
links, and that it wasn’t actually encouraging or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:30.840
enabling piracy. If you want, you could use the&nbsp;
site to share opensource software. While this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:36.560
technically true, I mean, let’s face it, these&nbsp;
guys were literally part of Piratbyrån and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:42.920
of this was completely intentional. So, their&nbsp;
argument was quickly shut down and on April 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:48.040
2009, all four of them would be sentenced to&nbsp;
one year in jail along with a 30 million kroner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:54.520
fine which is about $3.5 million. They would try&nbsp;
appealing and this did result in shorter prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.520 --> 00:15:00.160
sentences. Fredrick would be down to 10 months,&nbsp;
Peter would be down to 8 months, and Carl would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:08.000
down to 4 months. But, the appeal also came with a&nbsp;
larger fine of 46 million Kroner or $6.5 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:22.901
The walls were closing in, and there was only&nbsp;
one thing that the trio could do: get on the run.

00:15:22.901 --> 00:15:24.400
UNSTOPPABLE LEGACY:
If you’re wanted by Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:29.480
at large for causing billions of dollars worth of&nbsp;
lost revenue, you would think that it would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:36.680
good to lay low. But all three of our protagonists&nbsp;
would do the exact opposite. Fredrick would run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:41.920
away to Laos/Thailand and he would create yet&nbsp;
another file sharing site called BayFiles in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:48.800
2011. Apparently, this website was supposed to&nbsp;
comply with copyright rules, but you can take that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:54.280
for what you will. Not only did Fredrick launch&nbsp;
another site, but he would regularly cross the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.280 --> 00:16:00.000
border between Laos and Thailand because he had&nbsp;
residences in both countries. In fact, he crossed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.920
the border almost 30 times before getting caught&nbsp;
in November of 2014 at which point he would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:11.600
deported back to Sweden where he spent 200 days&nbsp;
in jail. Atleast Fredrick created another side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:17.320
that was legal atleast on the front. Gottfrid on&nbsp;
the other hand would run away to Cambodia and go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:24.320
full on spy mode and became a regular hacker for&nbsp;
Wikileaks. He would literally infiltrate Denmark’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:29.960
social security and driver’s license databases. I&nbsp;
don’t think that you’d be surprised to hear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:35.920
when he was caught in August of 2012, his prison&nbsp;
sentence would be upped to three and a half years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:42.200
but he would be let go after 3 years. And&nbsp;
finally as for Peter, well, my man straight up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:47.760
joined Finland’s Pirate party and ran for European&nbsp;
Parliament in the 2014 election. Let’s just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

